// Formative practice quiz for stacks-and-queues. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: collections-streams/collections-in-depth/stacks-and-queues

::stacks-and-queues o1 - push on a deque used as a queue::An empty ArrayDeque of strings runs offer("x"), offer("y") and then push("z"). What does poll() return next?{
=z#Correct. push adds at the head, and poll removes from the head, so z comes out first.
~x#Not quite. x would be first if every element had been added at the tail. push adds at the head instead.
~y#Not quite. y is in the middle; poll always takes the head.
~null#Not quite. poll returns null only when the deque is empty.
}

::stacks-and-queues o1 - a stack after mixed calls::An empty ArrayDeque of integers used as a stack runs push(1), push(2), push(3), pop() and push(4). What does peek() return?{
=4#Correct. pop removed 3, then push put 4 on the head, and peek reads the head.
~3#Not quite. pop already removed 3.
~2#Not quite. 2 is under the newest element, 4.
~1#Not quite. 1 was pushed first, so it is at the bottom of the stack.
}

::stacks-and-queues o1 - peek does not remove::An empty ArrayDeque of strings runs offer("a"), offer("b") and then peek(). What does the deque contain afterwards, head first?{
=[a, b]#Correct. peek only looks at the head; it removes nothing.
~[b]#Not quite. That would follow poll, which removes the head. peek does not.
~[b, a]#Not quite. offer adds at the tail, so a stays at the head.
~[]#Not quite. Nothing was removed.
}

::stacks-and-queues o2 - element on an empty deque::What happens when element() is called on an empty ArrayDeque?{
=It throws NoSuchElementException#Correct. element belongs to the throwing family with remove and pop.
~It returns null#Not quite. That is peek, the non-throwing partner of element.
~It throws NullPointerException#Not quite. NullPointerException comes from adding null, not from reading an empty deque.
~It blocks until another thread adds an element#Not quite. ArrayDeque never blocks; that behavior belongs to blocking queues.
}

::stacks-and-queues o2 - draining a queue in a loop::A loop drains a queue with while ((job \= jobs.poll()) !\= null) and processes each job. Why poll rather than remove?{
=poll returns null when the queue is empty, which ends the loop without an exception#Correct. poll is the right choice when empty is a normal state you check for.
~remove only works on lists, not on queues#Not quite. Queue declares remove() too; it throws when the queue is empty.
~poll is faster because it does not remove the element#Not quite. poll does remove the head; peek is the one that leaves it.
~remove returns null too, so either works the same#Not quite. remove throws NoSuchElementException on an empty queue, so the loop would end with an exception.
}

::stacks-and-queues o2 - adding null::Why does offer(null) on an ArrayDeque throw NullPointerException?{
=ArrayDeque prohibits null elements, because null is what poll and peek return for empty#Correct. If null could be stored, poll returning null would be ambiguous.
~The deque is full#Not quite. Array deques have no capacity restrictions; they grow as needed.
~offer only accepts numbers#Not quite. An ArrayDeque can hold any object type, just not null.
~It does not throw; it stores null at the tail#Not quite. The ArrayDeque documentation states that null elements are prohibited.
}

::stacks-and-queues o3 - Deque over Stack::Which reason for choosing an ArrayDeque over java.util.Stack comes from the Java API documentation?{
=Deque gives a more complete and consistent set of LIFO operations, and ArrayDeque is likely faster than Stack as a stack#Correct. Both statements are in the Stack and ArrayDeque documentation.
~Stack was removed in Java SE 25#Not quite. Stack still exists; it is a legacy class, not a removed one.
~Stack is first-in-first-out, so it cannot act as a stack#Not quite. Stack is last-in-first-out; the issue is that Deque is the preferred interface.
~Only ArrayDeque has a push method#Not quite. Stack has push too; the documentation recommends Deque for its complete, consistent operations.
}
